WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.189
Tabular language models could unlock the
spreadsheets buried inside industrial companies,

00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:07.940
but they need labeled data,

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.829
and only scarce experts can annotate these
technical tables.

00:00:11.909 --> 00:00:16.043
Presented at NeurIPS 2022 by Ringsquandl and Koleva
from Siemens,

00:00:16.123 --> 00:00:22.006
this paper asks: can active learning cut that
labeling cost for sub-cell entity recognition?

00:00:22.086 --> 00:00:26.544
Their headline finding is that a batch-diverse
gradient method, BADGE,

00:00:26.624 --> 00:00:30.174
matches full-training performance with far fewer labels,

00:00:30.254 --> 00:00:33.610
while forcing maximum table diversity actually hurts.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.923
Industry runs on spreadsheets.

00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:41.089
Operators track equipment, sensors, and vessels in
loosely structured tables,

00:00:41.169 --> 00:00:48.335
and extracting that information automatically means
fine-tuning tabular language models on labeled examples.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:53.635
But these tables use highly technical language only
a few experts can annotate,

00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:55.782
so labeling gets expensive fast.

00:00:55.862 --> 00:00:59.741
The paper frames this as sub-cell named entity
recognition,

00:00:59.821 --> 00:01:02.424
a genuinely new active learning problem,

00:01:02.504 --> 00:01:06.450
since each cell carries multiple token-level labels
at once.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:12.505
Active learning is built for this setting:
plentiful unlabeled data and expensive labels,

00:01:12.585 --> 00:01:15.932
squeezing the most performance from every annotation.

00:01:16.012 --> 00:01:19.877
But big transformer models can't learn one example
at a time;

00:01:19.957 --> 00:01:22.592
they train in batches over several epochs,

00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:26.601
so the acquisition function must choose a whole
batch at once.

00:01:26.681 --> 00:01:30.675
Pure uncertainty sampling grabs very similar,
correlated cells,

00:01:30.755 --> 00:01:33.391
often from the same table, wasting budget.

00:01:33.471 --> 00:01:37.788
Yet no one had studied active learning with tabular
language models,

00:01:37.868 --> 00:01:39.340
the gap this work fills.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:45.622
The model is a tabular language model: a
transformer encoder that swaps vanilla attention

00:01:45.702 --> 00:01:50.461
for a row-column visibility matrix with within-cell
positional encoding,

00:01:50.541 --> 00:01:53.352
so every token is aware of the whole table.

00:01:53.432 --> 00:01:58.863
A decoder tags each token over four entity types:
equipment tags, equipment names,

00:01:58.943 --> 00:02:00.947
physical quantities, and units.

00:02:01.027 --> 00:02:03.568
On top sits pool-based active learning,

00:02:03.648 --> 00:02:08.206
repeatedly picking the most informative cells for
an expert to label.

00:02:08.286 --> 00:02:14.054
Four acquisition functions compete: MNLP scores
normalized log-probability uncertainty;

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.078
MNLP-plus forces round-robin table diversity;

00:02:17.158 --> 00:02:22.523
BADGE clusters per-cell gradient embeddings with
k-means-plus-plus for uncertain,

00:02:22.603 --> 00:02:25.480
diverse batches; and Rand samples uniformly.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:31.231
Evaluation uses a real industrial dataset, not an
academic benchmark.

00:02:31.311 --> 00:02:37.235
Spreadsheets from several plants were downsampled
to at most five rows each,

00:02:37.315 --> 00:02:43.081
and expert annotators labeled every cell with the
Prodigy span-based tool.

00:02:43.161 --> 00:02:47.742
A random split gives 55 training tables and 24 for
testing.

00:02:47.822 --> 00:02:53.272
The training set holds 4,774 cells but only about
1,100 entity labels,

00:02:53.352 --> 00:02:59.118
just 0.23 per cell, meaning roughly 77 percent of
cells contain no entity,

00:02:59.198 --> 00:03:02.910
an extreme imbalance typical of industrial data.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:09.792
Performance is micro-averaged F1 on the held-out
test set across active learning iterations.

00:03:09.872 --> 00:03:17.949
BADGE is the standout: it beats random selection
and even surpasses the ceiling from training on the full dataset,

00:03:18.029 --> 00:03:19.594
using far fewer labels.

00:03:19.674 --> 00:03:23.029
Pure uncertainty sampling with MNLP disappoints,

00:03:23.109 --> 00:03:28.180
edging past random only after around 500 labels,
and with high variance.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:34.190
Most striking, MNLP-plus, which forces maximum
table diversity, is the worst of all.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:38.619
The four acquisition functions effectively ablate
diversity,

00:03:38.699 --> 00:03:42.679
and the lesson is that the right amount matters
enormously.

00:03:42.759 --> 00:03:45.362
BADGE's built-in batch diversity helps,

00:03:45.442 --> 00:03:50.454
but MNLP-plus's forced maximum diversity hurts,
falling below even random.

00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:57.335
Tracking how many distinct tables each method draws
from explains why: MNLP fixates on a few tables,

00:03:57.415 --> 00:04:02.359
MNLP-plus spreads across as many as possible, and
BADGE lands in between.

00:04:02.439 --> 00:04:08.070
The goal isn't maximum diversity but a careful
trade-off with per-cell uncertainty.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:15.336
The lasting message: active learning really can
slash the expert-labeling cost of tabular language models,

00:04:15.416 --> 00:04:18.161
but only if diversity is handled with care.

00:04:18.241 --> 00:04:22.433
Cell-level acquisition with built-in batch
diversity, like BADGE,

00:04:22.513 --> 00:04:26.704
reaches full-training performance using a fraction
of the labels,

00:04:26.784 --> 00:04:30.056
while bluntly maximizing table diversity backfires.

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:34.787
Balance diversity against uncertainty rather than
maximize either alone.

00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:39.584
As the first work here, it flags two frontiers:
acquisition compute cost,

00:04:39.664 --> 00:04:41.490
and annotator cognitive load.
